#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156969 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "343f8efc-fdef-51dc-ad3f-fad1ab37bc88" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Diagonal Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "5cba0e13-0b34-568b-8a69-eac3fced6338" :question/math-academy-id "e-16515" :question/problem "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}100 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$\nfind $\\log D$." :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a diagonal matrix $D$ with diagonal entries $\\lambda_1, \\ldots, \\lambda_n$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f(λ_{1}) & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & f(λ_{n})\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log D &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\log (100) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\log (1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\log (10)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156970 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ce12d6f-8d1c-52f3-992f-534b9d91dda9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Block"}] :question/id #uuid "f2d8972a-4337-5608-9c82-6fb5e74d22c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-16516" :question/problem "Find $f\\left(J_4(1) \\right)$ for $f(x)=2^x$." :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block $J_n(\\lambda)$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) & (f^{″}(λ))/(2!) & ⋯ & (f^{(n - 1)}(λ))/((n - 1)!) \\\\ 0 & f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) & ⋯ & (f^{(n - 2)}(λ))/((n - 2)!) \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(λ)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2^{x}\n$$\n\n$n=4$, and $\\lambda=1$.\n\nSince $n=4$, we need to compute the first $4-1=3$ derivatives of $f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 2^{x} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= 2^{x}\\ln 2 \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= 2^{x}(\\ln 2)^{2} \\\\\nf^{‴}(x) &= 2^{x}(\\ln 2)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(J_{4}(1)) &= \\begin{bmatrix}f(1) & f^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}f^{″}(1) & \\frac{1}{3!}f^{‴}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1) & f^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}f^{″}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(1) & f^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2^{1} & 2^{1}(\\ln 2) & \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2^{1}(\\ln 2)^{2} & \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 2^{1}(\\ln 2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & 2^{1} & 2^{1}(\\ln 2) & \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2^{1}(\\ln 2)^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2^{1} & 2^{1}(\\ln 2) \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2^{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2\\ln 2 & (\\ln 2)^{2} & \\frac{1}{3}(\\ln 2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\ln 2 & (\\ln 2)^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\ln 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156971 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "706303c1-0e4c-55f7-8b62-5094938f85bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "6b751a5e-56c2-5ef0-a640-b77793aa1a72" :question/math-academy-id "e-16517" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that $V = PJP^{-1}$, find $\\sin\\left(\\pi V\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S$, where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SAS^{-1}) = S \\cdot f(A) \\cdot S^{-1}\n$$\n\nFor\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sin(\\pi x)\n$$\n\nand the given matrix $V$, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin(\\pi V) = \\sin\\left(\\pi{PJP^{-1}}\\right) = P \\cdot (\\sin(\\pi J)) \\cdot P^{-1}\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $-3$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(-3) & f^{′}(-3) \\\\ 0 & f(-3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\pi \\cos \\pi x\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(-3) & f^{′}(-3) \\\\ 0 & f(-3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (πV) &= P \\cdot (\\sin (πJ)) \\cdot P^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & - π \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}π & - π \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}π & - π \\\\ π & - π\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156972 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87883002-67eb-5111-b15a-c5941bb33f9b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "2bd13883-8376-54f1-9985-83b95f1062a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-16522" :question/problem "Let $J_n\\left(\\lambda\\right)$ denote the $n \\times n$ Jordan block matrix with $\\lambda$ on the main diagonal. Then, find\n$e^{2\\left(J_2(1) {\\textstyle\\:\\oplus\\:} J_2(3)\\right)}$." :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block $J_n(\\lambda)$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) & (f^{″}(λ))/(2!) & ⋯ & (f^{(n - 1)}(λ))/((n - 1)!) \\\\ 0 & f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) & ⋯ & (f^{(n - 2)}(λ))/((n - 2)!) \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(λ) & f^{′}(λ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(λ)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{2(J_{2}(1)⊕J_{2}(3))} &= e^{2(\\begin{bmatrix}J_{2}(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & J_{2}(3)\\end{bmatrix})} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2(J_{2}(1))} & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{2(J_{2}(3))}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{2(J_{2}(1))}⊕e^{2(J_{2}(3))}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For $J_2(1)$, we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda=1$. Since $n=2$, we need to compute the first derivative of $f$ only:\n$f(x)|= e^{2x}; f^{′}(x)|= 2e^{2x}$\nSo, we get\n$f(J_{2}(1))|= \\begin{bmatrix}f(1) & f^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2 \\cdot 1} & 2e^{2 \\cdot 1} \\\\ 0 & e^{2 \\cdot 1}\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2} & 2e^{2} \\\\ 0 & e^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n- For $J_2(3)$, we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda=3$. Since $n=2$, we need the first derivative of $f$ only, which we have already computed. So, we get\n$f(J_{2}(3))|= \\begin{bmatrix}f(3) & f^{′}(3) \\\\ 0 & f(3)\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2 \\cdot 3} & 2e^{2 \\cdot 3} \\\\ 0 & e^{2 \\cdot 3}\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{6} & 2e^{6} \\\\ 0 & e^{6}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{2} & 2e^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & e^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & e^{6} & 2e^{6} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & e^{6}\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "343f8efc-fdef-51dc-ad3f-fad1ab37bc88" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Diagonal Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "a4e6fc73-108c-50c4-9514-9d59798d2970" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154651} {:answer-field/id #uuid "ad4d723b-43bd-5599-ae4a-eb150d4d50df" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154652} {:answer-field/id #uuid "c602b169-076c-51ab-9bad-a28a0315739d" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154653} {:answer-field/id #uuid "c9c1d064-3747-5828-859e-14cdf91454cd" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154654} {:answer-field/id #uuid "3d706219-8991-5ee5-9591-17c978fd81f8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154655} {:answer-field/id #uuid "5896e7dd-a1cc-536e-8218-97d62adba84a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154656}] :question/id #uuid "ac5e267a-3d39-53ad-978a-d448d4a58398" :question/math-academy-id "q-252924" :question/problem "Given that\n$D = \\begin{bmatrix}π & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & - π & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2π\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind $\\cos D$.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\cos D =$ |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ce12d6f-8d1c-52f3-992f-534b9d91dda9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Block"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "a4165b09-9df9-52e7-ac8e-9def3363712b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154657} {:answer-field/id #uuid "ad3e0799-1ba8-5255-a4fa-075f7fe3680c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154658} {:answer-field/id #uuid "066822de-d8ec-5dc2-84dd-c66366b93ff4" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154659} {:answer-field/id #uuid "3cfc6554-c36e-5035-bb84-e33cc23e41bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154660}] :question/id #uuid "4142f862-e575-5cdf-acae-f1cf787219fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-252925" :question/problem "Let $J_{n}(λ)$ denote an $n \\cdot n$ Jordan block with $λ$ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | $6$ | {{field-1}} | $-3$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\sin (J_{4}(\\frac{π}{6})) = \\frac{1}{12}$ |  | $0$ | $6$ | {{field-3}} | $-3$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ |  |\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"computing-the-value-of-a-function-of-a-jordan-matrix\"></a>"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87883002-67eb-5111-b15a-c5941bb33f9b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field